meta {
  name: PostgreSQL Event Sourcing API
  type: collection
  seq: 1
}

auth {
  mode: none
}

docs {
  # PostgreSQL Event Sourcing — API collection

  Full REST surface of the `event-sourcing-app` sample (Spring Boot 4 / Java 25),
  covering the happy paths, the idempotency contract, bean validation, the
  domain invariants, the admin/operations endpoints and the actuator metrics.

  ## Prerequisites

  The stack must be up:

  ```bash
  docker compose up -d --scale event-sourcing-app=2
  ```

  The API is served through nginx on `http://localhost:8080` (two app instances
  behind the load balancer, so concurrent requests really do hit two different
  JVMs).

  ## Import

  1. Bruno -> File -> Import Collection -> select the `bruno` folder.
  2. Select the `Local` environment (it holds `baseUrl`, `riderId`, `driverId`).

  ## Folders

  | Folder | What it covers |
  | --- | --- |
  | `01 - Happy Path` | Create order, query it, adjust price, accept, complete, cancel |
  | `02 - Idempotency` | RFC 9440 / Stripe replay, key mismatch, missing key |
  | `03 - Validation Errors` | Bean validation and domain input validation (400) |
  | `04 - Domain Invariants` | Illegal state transitions of the order aggregate |
  | `05 - Not Found & Malformed` | 404 and malformed path/JSON payloads |
  | `06 - Admin & Operations` | Projection rebuild, dead letter queue |
  | `07 - Actuator Monitoring` | Health, metrics, Prometheus scrape |

  ## Running

  Run a folder (not a single request) for `01 - Happy Path`, `02 - Idempotency`
  and `04 - Domain Invariants` — the later requests depend on variables captured
  by the earlier ones (`orderId`, `idemOrderId`, `domainOrderId`, ...).

  The happy path uses a fresh `{{$guid}}` idempotency key on every run, so each
  run creates a brand new order. The idempotency folder generates a fresh key
  in the pre-request script, so the first call is always a real first call and
  the next requests exercise the replay path. Both stay green when the
  collection is run repeatedly.
}

vars:pre-request {
  baseUrl: http://localhost:8080
}
